one of indiana's largest employers is  sharing its concerns after the state  passed a near total ban on abortions now  indiana is the first state to pass such  a measure since the supreme court  overturned roe v wade the pharmaceutical  giant that's eli lilly and company warn  this we will be forced to plan for more  employment growth outside our home state  as state laws change across the country  now doctors are becoming increasingly  concerned they're very worried about  what they can and cannot do for their  patients so we spoke to four obgyns from  arizona wisconsin ohio and georgia about  how state abortion laws create a lot of  confusion and all of them expressed  concerns and new laws will put their  patients health at risk  as soon as we heard the decision we  closed everything down leaving over 70  women in wisconsin with appointments  that we weren't able to  do for them  we've been preparing it didn't make it  any more devastating i remember asking  when is the law going to change how much  time do we have to get ready for it and  they started to text back and say the  law has actually changed right now i've  lived in the south for most of my life  this is my community  and i'm very concerned about the ability  of my patients my community to access  the care that we need when medicine is  criminalized physicians are scared to do  the jobs they're trained to do  and that absolutely impacts patient care  people are very quick to talk about how  these abortion restrictions have medical  emergency  exceptions but for us as clinicians  actually practicing on the ground those  exceptions just don't really make sense  now many of those options have been  taken away  and  that is just unbearable for all of us  some women will have what we call a  spontaneous miscarriage where they start  to miscarry on their own we have to  counsel them that they may get very sick  that they may develop a life-threatening  infection  and it could potentially cost them their  lives you can't make a one-size-fits-all  definition or law that can take all of  these situations into account we're  seeing issues in labor and delivery for  example of women with pre-viable  pregnancies that come in with infection  that need to be delivered and so there's  a lot of back and forth as to how sick  does the patient need to be to get  delivered does she have to develop an  infection can we intervene before she  develops an infection how sick does she  have to be  before the staff felt comfortable  delivering her because of this law  hanging over our head  if someone who's pregnant comes in after  getting in a car accident or comes in  with an inflamed appendix  are doctors going to be afraid to  get imaging to take them to surgery if  they need surgery because with those  treatments  there is a chance that you could  inadvertently  cause an abortion what if someone has  cancer and they're not gonna die right  then but because they don't receive  abortion care they don't receive the  usual cancer care that they would  receive and thus they die from the  cancer five years later  is that a life that we could have saved  doctors are having to figure out  does this fit under the law am i going  to be criminalized for intervening  i think physicians are afraid that they  will be accused  of providing abortion care when really  they're providing miscarriage management  i think my greatest fear is  how our patients are going to be able to  get the care they need it means so much  to me to be able to take care of my  patients  and  they are suffering because of this  suffering economically suffering  their families are suffering  their futures  will suffer  but some of them are going to die  and they could be saved that's a  travesty  another big concern is misinformation  the doctors say one of them we spoke to  said she heard from a pregnant patient  who is too afraid to get the medical  care why because she fears that her  health will be sacrificed for her un